{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC1 design decision, with the measurement that settled it.\n\nBoth branches AC1 permits were investigated. Took branch (a) — the case declares acceptability — and rejected branch (b), scoring a later pipeline stage.\n\nWhy Pass 1 cannot carry the distinction, measured rather than assumed: the union of keys on real Pass-1 findings captured in the e508 diagnostic run is {checklist_item, criteria, evidence, finding, impact, location, scenarios, suggested_fix}. `impact` looked like a severity candidate, so I read its value on the captured finding: it is an empty free-text string, not a graded axis. That matches the rubric's own contract (code-review-tests.md:6 \"No model-emitted severity (computed deterministically in Pass 3)\" and :142 \"Do NOT emit severity/confidence/priority\").\n\nWhy branch (b) was rejected: `pass3_decide` (decide.py:585) returns INDETERMINATE without a `verification` block, so reaching the stage where severity exists requires running Pass 2 — another LLM call per case. `impact_code` (decide.py:545) consumes the verifier's consequence binaries (silent_failure, escapes_automation, trigger_likelihood, prod_impact, churn90, ...), none of which exist on a Pass-1 finding. Beyond the cost, it makes the eval a weaker oracle for the thing it exists to calibrate: a Pass-1 finder regression would be hidden whenever Pass 2 or 3 happened to drop the finding.\n\nImplementation: new `ADVISORY_EXPECTS = frozenset({\"advisory\"})` in eval_scorers.py, folded into ALLOWED_EXPECTS. `_no_fire` stands down on it (documented in its docstring); eval.py already routes any non-fire/no-fire expect into the \"run it, don't score it in the calibration view\" path, so the case still executes live and its output is still held to the grounding scorers. It is not a vacuous pass.\n\nThe new expectation has a real user rather than being dead vocabulary: FP-preexisting-code-gap-no-new-behaviour. That case's own note already said \"guard (vii) keeps that advisory\" while its `expect: pass` demanded the opposite — the fixture was internally contradictory. It is now `expect: advisory`, and the note says why.\n\nGuard (vii) is unchanged, per AC1's last sentence. FP-deliberate-tightening passes by having its own second defect fixed instead: it tested only the rejection path, so the reviewer had a genuine coverage gap to flag. Added the happy-path assertion. Guard (vii) now never engages there.\n\nAC2: the loc-ceiling mirror diff now imports SRC_ROOT/gate_file_loc/read_limit and defines line_counts (it previously referenced an undefined name and would have raised NameError).\nAC3: the rule-5 glob loop now materializes the glob and asserts non-emptiness, so it cannot pass vacuously.\nAC4: the pre-existing-code diff no longer adds a new public `format_release_tag`; it adds only a docstring to a two-year-old function, so it introduces no new observable behaviour and no longer contradicts its own note.\nAC6 verified: epochs 3, gate at_least(2), coverage_threshold 0.8, model unchanged; 33 cases before and after, no `- id:` line added or removed.\n\n`make lint` exits 0, including the complexity, mechanism-delta and module-size ratchets. Offline suite: tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py + test_new_eval_specs.py + test_plan_review_novelty.py, 49 passed.\n\nAC5 (full live 3-epoch eval, local gate_source, AWS_PROFILE=frontier) is running now. AC7 `make verify` follows."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793817821220789144,"uuid":"af542320-9834-421b-ab10-ced32c9ee7a5"}